From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 27 May 2026 15:01:43 -0700
Subject: [PATCH RFC 0/3] net: flow_dissector: fast-path for common L3+L4 shapes

Hi all,

This series adds a fast-path to __skb_flow_dissect() for the
most common packet shapes seen on Linux RX paths:

  - eth + IPv4 + TCP or UDP
  - eth + IPv6 + TCP or UDP

On a match the fast-path produces byte-identical struct
flow_keys output and returns. On a miss it falls back to the
existing slow-path graph walk.

This is the third RFC I have sent to netdev. The first two
(flow_hash_from_keys_small + sch_cake adoption, and bpf_flow
PPPoE support) came out of the same comparison work led by Tom
Herbert (https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2). XDP2 shows
that a straight-line "decision tree at function entry" extractor
for common shapes saves 35-42 % over the slow-path graph walk
on the same packets. This series brings that one technique to
the in-tree dissector as plain C.


The patches
===========

  1/3  net: flow_dissector: add fast-path entry-point skeleton
       - Adds the flow_dissect_fast() dispatcher.
       - No fast-path arms yet; per-shape helpers are stubs.
       - Reject custom dissectors and slow-path-only flags.
       - Cost on a miss: one ethertype switch (about 5 cycles).

  2/3  net: flow_dissector: add eth+IPv4+{TCP,UDP} fast-path
       - Plug the IPv4 stub.
       - Reject IP options, fragments, other L4 protocols.
       - Expected saving: about 30 ns/pkt on matching packets.

  3/3  net: flow_dissector: add eth+IPv6+{TCP,UDP} fast-path
       - Plug the IPv6 stub.
       - Reject extension headers.
       - Defer when the caller asks for the flow label and the
         label is non-zero (uncommon on real traffic).
       - Expected saving: about 30 ns/pkt on matching packets.

Total added code: about 207 lines, all in
net/core/flow_dissector.c. Uses C11 idioms supported by the
kernel since 5.18 (-std=gnu11): static_assert for header-size
invariants, unlikely() hints for the fast-path bail-out
branches.


What this is NOT
================

  - NOT a new dissector API. __skb_flow_dissect() signature
    and contract are unchanged.
  - NOT a struct flow_keys layout change. The struct is ABI for
    the BPF flow_dissector hook.
  - NOT a change to BPF flow_dissector precedence. The fast-path
    runs only when the existing BPF override check decides no
    BPF program is loaded for this netns.
  - NOT VLAN-aware in v1. VLAN-tagged packets fall through to
    the slow path. A VLAN fast-path is a planned follow-up.
  - NOT an XDP2 framework upstream. This series is hand-written
    C for one technique only. The XDP2 framework conversation
    is a separate, larger topic.


Output byte-equivalence
=======================

The fast-path writes the same key slots the slow path writes
for the standard dissectors on the matching shape:

  - key_control: addr_type, thoff, flags
  - key_basic: n_proto, ip_proto
  - key_addrs.v{4,6}addrs: src and dst from the IP header
  - key_ports: src and dst u16 ports at the L4 header

Cold key slots that the slow path does not write for this
shape (KEY_IP, KEY_TCP, KEY_VLAN, KEY_FLOW_LABEL when zero,
etc.) are not in the standard dissector's key list. They stay
at the caller's pre-memset zero.

A follow-up selftest will verify byte-by-byte equivalence on a
packet corpus.


Why the kernel cannot capture every optimisation XDP2 shows
===========================================================

XDP2 runs at about 71 ns/pkt on these workloads. The in-tree
dissector with this series should reach about 85-90 ns/pkt
(from 117 today). The remaining ~15 ns gap is structural:

  - always_inline plus LTO into the caller. XDP2's parser
    inlines into the test harness as one block. The kernel
    cannot do this: flow_dissector.o and sch_cake.o are
    different translation units, and the function is too
    large to inline.
  - Constant folding of static const proto_def fields. XDP2
    folds at compile time. The kernel reads runtime structs.
  - Per-callsite specialisation. XDP2 can emit a fresh parser
    per use-site. flow_dissector serves many callers.

These are the price of being a generic kernel API rather than
a per-callsite specialised parser. The technique this series
backports captures most of the win and pays this price for
the rest.


Prior art
=========

Earlier wholesale-replacement attempts for flow_dissector have
stalled. This series intentionally takes the opposite path:
incremental, no new framework, no new tooling, no new ABI.

  - 2017 Tom Herbert, "Protocol specific flow dissector
    offload":
    https://patchwork.ozlabs.org/project/netdev/cover/20170928235230.22158-1-tom@quantonium.net/
  - 2021 Tom Herbert, "Replacing Flow Dissector with PANDA
